## Rules Without a Guard Hook Here

Claude Code and Cursor enforce these with hooks. This agent runs none of them, so this text is the only guard and you must apply it yourself before every write and every reply.

### Comments

A comment that stays states a reason the code cannot show; words like `because`, `to avoid`, `workaround for`, `invariant:` or `lock ordering` usually appear in one, but adding them to narration does not make it a reason. Standard directives are fine: `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `# pylint:`, `# shellcheck disable=`, `// eslint-disable-next-line`, `// @ts-expect-error`, `// prettier-ignore`, `//go:generate`, `//nolint`, `#pragma`, `#[derive(...)]`, `@param`, `@return`, `TODO:`, `FIXME:`, `NOTE:`, `HACK:`, `SAFETY:`, `INVARIANT:`, shebangs.

### Shortcut language

Before sending a reply, check it for phrases like "simpler approach", "simpler way", "simpler solution", "quick fix", "quick and dirty", "good enough for now", "let me just", "let's just", "for now I'll", "hack together", "band-aid", "workaround for now", "low-hanging fruit", "easy way out", "minimal change", "stub out for now", "naive approach", "poor man's", "punt on", "for simplicity" or "throw-away version". Each one marks a hard problem being dodged: name that problem and commit to the real work instead of rewording the sentence.
